# x11: 20 new guides (part 4)
try: FAQX
except NameError: FAQX={}; METAX={}
LONG.update({
'make-your-own-album-background-psd':('How to Make Your Own Album Background PSD',[
"Ready-made backgrounds are convenient, but sooner or later a client wants a colour or mood no template offers. Building your own album background in Photoshop is simpler than it looks, and a small personal library of backgrounds makes every future album faster. This guide walks through the process for a 12x36 sheet.",
"## Set up the document",
"Create a new document at 36 x 12 inches, 300 pixels per inch, RGB colour. That gives 10800 x 3600 pixels. Add guides at 18 inches for the fold and 0.5 inch from each edge for the safe area. Our [12x36 album size guide](/blog/12x36-album-size-guide/) has the details for other sizes.",
"## Step 1: Choose a base colour",
"Pick a colour from the wedding itself: the bride's saree, the stage decoration or the invitation. Use the Eyedropper on a reference photo, then lighten or desaturate it. Backgrounds should support photos, not compete with them, so softer versions of strong colours usually work best. Our [wedding album colour themes](/blog/wedding-album-colour-themes/) guide offers palettes.",
"## Step 2: Add depth with gradients",
"A flat colour looks digital. Add a Gradient Fill layer from the base colour to a slightly darker or warmer tone. Radial gradients that are lighter in the centre of each page draw attention to photos. Keep the change subtle, around 10 to 15 percent difference in brightness.",
"### Prevent banding",
"Smooth gradients can show stripes when printed. Add Filter > Noise > Add Noise at 0.5 to 1 percent, Gaussian, Monochromatic, on a merged copy or a separate noise layer set to Overlay.",
"## Step 3: Add texture",
"Texture makes a background feel like paper or fabric. Options include:",
"- **Paper texture:** scan or photograph a sheet of handmade paper and blend it with Multiply or Soft Light at low opacity.",
"- **Fabric texture:** a close-up photo of silk or linen, desaturated and set to Overlay.",
"- **Bokeh:** a blurred photo of fairy lights for reception backgrounds.",
"Only use textures you have created or have the right to use commercially.",
"## Step 4: Add ornaments and patterns",
"### Simple shapes",
"Thin lines, corner flourishes and borders drawn with the Pen tool or custom shapes add elegance. Keep them away from where photos will sit.",
"### Pattern overlays",
"Define a small motif as a pattern (Edit > Define Pattern), then apply it with a Pattern Fill layer at 5 to 10 percent opacity. Traditional motifs like paisleys and temple borders suit Indian weddings.",
"### Golden effects",
"For gold ornaments, apply a Gradient Overlay layer style from dark brown through yellow to pale cream, plus a subtle Bevel and Emboss. It prints far more convincingly than plain yellow.",
"## Step 5: Leave room for photos",
"Sketch where photos will sit and keep those areas calm. Decorative detail belongs in margins and corners. Test by placing two or three real photos before finishing.",
"## Step 6: Organise and save",
"Name groups clearly as Base, Texture, Ornaments and Guides. Save the layered PSD in a backgrounds library. Save a flattened JPG version too, for quick use. Making variations is then easy: duplicate, change the base colour with Hue/Saturation, and save.",
"## Building matching sets",
"An album needs several backgrounds that feel related. Create one master, then make variations with different gradient directions, ornament positions or lighter and darker tones. Using one design family throughout gives the album a professional consistency.",
"## When to use ready-made backgrounds",
"Making backgrounds takes time. For tight deadlines, start from a free design in the [album background category](/category/album-background/) or the [wedding album background PSD tag](/tag/wedding-album-background-psd/) and adjust colour. Our [guide to choosing album backgrounds](/blog/choose-album-background-psd/) helps you pick. For studio portraits rather than albums, see the [studio background category](/category/studio-background/) and [studio backdrop PSD guide](/blog/studio-backdrop-psd-guide/). Then place your sheets in the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/) templates to compare.",
]),
'wedding-album-captions-and-quotes':('Wedding Album Captions and Quotes: A Guide',[
"Text on album pages should be short, meaningful and easy to read. A good caption or quote adds emotion; a bad one feels like clip art. This guide covers where to use text in a wedding album, how to write it, and original example lines you can adapt.",
"## Where text belongs in an album",
"- **Cover and title page:** names, date and sometimes the venue.",
"- **Chapter openers:** short titles such as The Engagement, Haldi, The Ceremony, Reception.",
"- **Key moments:** a line beside the garland exchange, the first look or the vidaai.",
"- **Closing page:** a thank-you or a blessing.",
"Most spreads need no text at all. Too many quotes make the album feel like a greeting card.",
"## Writing good captions",
"### Keep it personal",
"The best text comes from the couple. Ask how they met, a phrase they use with each other, or a line from their vows. Even a single detail, such as the city where they first met, makes a caption memorable.",
"### Keep it short",
"Aim for under twelve words for most captions. Longer passages belong on a single dedication page.",
"### Match the tone",
"A traditional temple wedding suits a respectful, warm tone. A destination wedding or modern reception can be more playful.",
"## Original example lines",
"Here are lines you can adapt. Change details to fit the couple.",
"- **Cover:** Two families, one story. Or simply the names and date in an elegant script.",
"- **Engagement:** The day we said yes to forever.",
"- **Getting ready:** Every detail, chosen with love.",
"- **Ceremony:** Seven steps, a lifetime of promises.",
"- **Garland exchange:** And just like that, we were family.",
"- **Family portraits:** The people who made us who we are.",
"- **Reception:** Then we danced.",
"- **Closing:** Thank you for being part of our beginning.",
"Avoid copying famous poems or song lyrics in full; short original lines are safer and more personal.",
"## Regional language text",
"Many families appreciate a line in Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam, Kannada, Hindi or Urdu. Ask a family member to write or check the text, because a spelling error in a printed album is permanent. Make sure the font renders the script correctly; see [how to fix missing fonts in a PSD file](/blog/fix-missing-fonts-in-psd/).",
"## Typography for captions",
"Use the album's main font pair: a script for titles, a clean serif or sans for captions. Keep caption size around 18 to 28 points on a 12x36 sheet, with comfortable line spacing. Place text on calm areas of the background, never over busy ornaments or faces. Our [fonts for wedding albums](/blog/fonts-for-wedding-albums/) guide covers pairing.",
"### Readability checks",
"Print a test or view at 100 percent zoom. Thin scripts in light colours can disappear on gold backgrounds; add a subtle shadow or choose a heavier weight.",
"## Text in other albums",
"The same principles apply to [anniversary albums](/blog/anniversary-album-design-ideas/), [puberty ceremony albums](/blog/puberty-ceremony-album-design/) and [birthday albums](/blog/birthday-album-psd-ideas/), which often carry a little more text.",
"## Templates with text layers",
"Many free templates include editable text placeholders. Browse the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/), the [album cover category](/category/album-cover/) and the [wedding invitation category](/category/wedding-invitation/), where invitation wording can inspire album captions.",
]),
'parents-album-design-guide':('Parents Album Design: Smaller Copies Done Right',[
"Many couples order smaller albums for their parents, sometimes one for each side of the family. A parents' album is not just a shrunken copy of the main album; done well, it is a thoughtful edit focused on what parents care about. This guide explains how to plan, select and design parents' albums efficiently.",
"## Why parents' albums differ",
"Couples usually want romantic portraits and candid moments with friends. Parents often care more about rituals, relatives and family portraits. A parents' album that reflects this is far more appreciated, and it is a valuable upsell for studios.",
"## Choosing the size",
"Common sizes are 12x30, 12x24 or 10x30 spreads, or 12x18 sheets, depending on budget. Smaller formats are lighter for older hands to hold. Keep the same proportions as the main album if you want to reuse layouts directly. See [wedding album sizes in India](/blog/wedding-album-sizes-india/) and the [12x18 album size guide](/blog/12x18-album-size-guide/).",
"## Selecting photos",
"- **Prioritise rituals:** ceremony moments in sequence.",
"- **Family portraits:** every important relative from that side of the family.",
"- **Their moments:** the parents with the couple, blessing scenes, the kanyadaan or equivalent.",
"- **Fewer couple portraits:** keep the best few, not the whole shoot.",
"- **Guests:** elders and relatives the parents will want to show visitors.",
"Ask each set of parents for a short list of must-include people. It prevents disappointment later.",
"## Adapting layouts from the main album",
"### Same aspect ratio",
"If the parents' album uses the same proportions, for example 12x36 main and 10x30 parents, you can scale the finished sheets with Image Size. Text and ornaments shrink proportionally, so check that captions remain readable.",
"### Different aspect ratio",
"If sizes differ, such as 12x36 to 12x30, scaling alone distorts the design. Rebuild the sheet using the same background and photos, and adjust frames. Our guide to [resizing album PSDs](/blog/resize-album-psd-to-another-size/) covers both cases.",
"## Design considerations for older viewers",
"Older viewers appreciate larger faces and simpler layouts. Use fewer photos per spread, bigger family portraits and clear, readable fonts for names. Avoid very dark or very busy backgrounds that make photos hard to see in ordinary room lighting.",
"## Two families, two albums",
"For the bride's and groom's families, the albums may share many ceremony photos but differ in family portraits and some events. Build a master set of shared spreads, then add family-specific spreads. Name files clearly, for example Bride_Parents_05 and Groom_Parents_05, to avoid mix-ups at the lab.",
"## Pricing and workflow",
"Because much of the design work is reused, parents' albums can be offered at an attractive price while still being profitable. Agree the photo selection with the couple and parents before design to limit revisions; see [managing album revisions](/blog/album-design-client-revisions/).",
"## Templates to start from",
"Browse the [12x30 album category](/category/album-12x30/), the [12x18 album category](/category/album-12x18/) and the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/) for matching designs. A simple cover from the [album cover category](/category/album-cover/) completes the set. For the main album structure, see [how many pages a wedding album needs](/blog/how-many-pages-in-a-wedding-album/).",
]),
'album-binding-and-lamination-guide':('Album Binding and Lamination Guide for 12x36',[
"Design is only half of a finished album. The binding and lamination you choose change how the album opens, how photos across the fold look, and how long it lasts. This guide explains the common options for 12x36 albums so you can design for them and advise clients clearly.",
"## How a 12x36 album is made",
"Each designed 12x36 spread is printed on photo paper, then mounted or laminated, and the sheets are bound together with a cover. The 12x36 spread folds into a 12x18 book. Different binding methods handle the fold differently, which affects how you design.",
"## Common binding types",
"### Lay-flat binding",
"Each sheet is mounted on a thick board and hinged so the spread opens completely flat. Photos can cross the centre with minimal loss, making it ideal for panoramic layouts. Karizma-style albums are typically lay-flat with thick sheets.",
"### Flush-mount albums",
"Prints are mounted on rigid pages with no visible gap, giving a premium, solid feel. They open nearly flat. Treat the fold as a soft area and avoid faces there.",
"### Conventional photo book binding",
"Thinner pages glued or sewn at the spine. The centre curves into the binding, so anything at the fold may be hidden. Design with a wider gutter, about 0.5 to 0.75 inch on each side of the fold.",
"## Lamination options",
"- **Matte:** no glare, hides fingerprints, soft and elegant. Popular for modern and pastel albums.",
"- **Glossy:** vibrant colours and deep blacks, but shows reflections and fingerprints.",
"- **Textured finishes:** canvas, silk, sparkle or 3D textures add a tactile feel. Fine text and small details can look less sharp under strong textures.",
"- **Velvet or soft-touch:** a smooth, premium finish that suits dark, rich designs.",
"Ask the lab for a sample book showing finishes on real prints, as names vary between labs. Our [paper finishes guide](/blog/best-paper-for-wedding-albums/) covers paper choices.",
"## Designing for the binding",
"- **Know the method before designing:** request the lab's template or gutter guidance.",
"- **Keep a safe zone at the fold:** even lay-flat albums have a small hinge area.",
"- **Bleed and trim:** sheets are trimmed after lamination, so extend backgrounds to the edge and keep text 0.25 to 0.5 inch inside.",
"- **Sheet count affects the spine:** finalise the number of sheets before designing the cover. See [album cover dimensions](/blog/album-cover-dimensions/).",
"## How lamination affects colour",
"Glossy lamination makes colours richer and darker; matte lifts blacks slightly and softens contrast. If you know the finish, adjust: slightly more contrast for matte, slightly lighter shadows for glossy. A test print on the chosen finish is the best guide.",
"## Durability",
"Thick boards and quality lamination protect against bending, moisture and fingerprints, which matters for albums passed around at family gatherings. Advise clients to store albums flat or upright in a box, away from direct sunlight and humidity.",
"## Explaining options to clients",
"Clients often choose based on a sample. Keep two or three sample albums showing your recommended combinations and explain how each affects price; our article on [Karizma album printing cost factors](/blog/karizma-album-printing-cost-factors/) helps here.",
"## Prepare files correctly",
"Before printing, follow the [print-ready PSD checklist](/blog/print-ready-psd-checklist/) and [export sheets as JPG for print](/blog/export-album-sheets-jpg-for-print/). Design with templates from the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/), the [Karizma album PSD tag](/tag/karizma-album-psd/) and the [album cover category](/category/album-cover/).",
]),
'album-design-client-revisions':('Managing Album Design Revisions with Clients',[
"Endless revisions are the biggest drain on an album designer's time. A clear process for proofs and feedback keeps clients happy, protects your schedule and gets albums to the lab faster. This guide covers practical steps for managing album revisions professionally.",
"## Set expectations before design",
"Agree on the essentials at the start: album size, number of sheets, number of revision rounds, and the timeline. Two rounds of changes are common; extra rounds can carry a fee. Put this in writing, even in a simple message, so both sides remember the agreement.",
"## Get photo selection approved first",
"Most revisions come from photo changes, not design. Ask the couple to approve or shortlist photos before you start layouts. Shared online galleries with favourites make this easy. Our guide on [organising photos before album design](/blog/organise-photos-before-album-design/) explains how to prepare.",
"### Ask for must-have lists",
"Request a list of must-include people and moments from the couple and both sets of parents. It prevents the late discovery that an important uncle is missing.",
"## Send clear proofs",
"### Use a PDF or low-resolution images",
"Combine sheets into a single PDF with page numbers, or send numbered JPGs at reduced size. Never send full-resolution print files as proofs. Our guide to [making a PDF for client approval](/blog/wedding-album-pdf-for-client-approval/) shows how.",
"### Watermark proofs",
"A subtle watermark discourages printing proofs elsewhere. See [how to add a studio logo or watermark](/blog/add-studio-logo-watermark-photoshop/).",
"## Collect feedback in one place",
"Ask the client to send all feedback in one message per round, listing the sheet number and the change, for example: Sheet 07, replace photo 3 with a group photo. Scattered voice notes and calls lead to missed changes.",
"### Use a simple change log",
"Keep a list of every requested change and tick it off as you make it. Send the list back with the next proof so the client can see everything was handled.",
"## Making changes efficiently",
"Organised PSDs make revisions fast. Keep photos as Smart Objects so you can use Replace Contents, and keep layers named. See [Smart Objects for album design](/blog/smart-objects-album-design/) and [how to speed up your album design workflow](/blog/speed-up-album-design-workflow/).",
"## Handling difficult requests",
"- **Too many photos per sheet:** explain that crowded pages make faces small, and offer an extra sheet instead.",
"- **Changing the whole style late:** this is a redesign, not a revision; discuss timeline and cost.",
"- **Conflicting feedback from family members:** ask the couple to confirm a single final list.",
"Stay polite and refer back to the agreement. Most clients appreciate a clear explanation.",
"## Final approval",
"Before sending to the lab, ask for written final approval of the last proof, including names, dates and spellings. Once approved, export print files following the [print-ready PSD checklist](/blog/print-ready-psd-checklist/) and [send files to the lab](/blog/send-album-files-to-lab/).",
"## Templates that make revisions easier",
"Templates with clearly named layers and separate photo frames are faster to revise. Try sheets from the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/), the [12x30 album category](/category/album-12x30/) and the [album background category](/category/album-background/). For more on running an album service, read [how to start an album design service from home](/blog/start-album-design-business/).",
]),
})
